<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Akinyi mixes <m>{6}</m> kg of maize flour at Ksh <m>{70}</m> a kilogram with <m>{2}</m> kg of millet flour at Ksh <m>{225}</m> a kilogram to make uji flour, and says: <q>The mixture costs <m>({70} + {225}) \div 2 = {147.5}</m> shillings a kilogram.</q></p><p>(a) Which statement is right?</p><p/><ol marker="(1)">
<li>Akinyi is wrong: a mixture always costs the same per kilogram as the dearer flour.</li>
<li>Akinyi is wrong: the mixture costs the sum of the prices, Ksh 295 a kilogram.</li>
<li>Akinyi is right: a mixture always costs the mean of the two prices.</li>
<li>Akinyi is wrong: more of the cheaper maize flour is used, so the cost per kilogram is nearer to the price of that flour.</li>
</ol>  <p>(b) What does 1 kg of the mixture cost, to the nearest shilling?</p><p>Ksh <fillin characters="6" name="ans2"/>  </p><p>(c) Keeping the <m>{6}</m> kg of maize flour, how many kilograms of millet flour would make Akinyi's statement true?</p><p><fillin characters="6" name="ans3"/> kg  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) and (b) Total cost: <m>{6} \times {70} + {2} \times {225} = {870}</m> shillings. Total mass: <m>{8}</m> kg. Cost of 1 kg: <m>{870} \div {8} = {108.75}</m>, so Ksh <m>{109}</m> to the nearest shilling. The cost of a mixture leans towards the ingredient you use more of: here more of the cheaper maize flour is used.</p><p>(c) The mean of the prices is right only when the two masses are equal, so <m>{6}</m> kg of millet flour. Check: <m>({6} \times {70} + {6} \times {225}) \div {12} = {147.5}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>(4)</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{435}{4}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>6</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>